-- ID 996: Advanced Basis Calculations — Effect of multiple partnership adjustments on outside basis ordering
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Martinez is a partner in Ridgemont Consulting LLC and begins the year with an outside basis of $50,000. During the year, the partnership allocates $35,000 of ordinary income, $12,000 of tax-exempt municipal bond interest, a $15,000 cash distribution, and $8,000 of charitable contributions to Martinez. How does the ordering of these basis adjustments affect Martinez''s year-end outside basis, and what would happen if the distribution had been $60,000 instead of $15,000?',
  choices = '["The ordering is irrelevant because all adjustments net together simultaneously, producing a basis of $74,000 either way", "Basis increases for income and tax-exempt items first ($50,000 + $35,000 + $12,000 = $97,000), then decreases for distributions and deductions ($97,000 - $15,000 - $8,000 = $74,000) — if the distribution were $60,000 instead, basis would still be $74,000 because distributions cannot reduce basis below zero and the excess is deferred", "Basis increases for income and tax-exempt items first ($97,000), then decreases for distributions and deductions ($74,000) — if the distribution were $60,000, the excess over remaining basis after deductions ($60,000 - $29,000 = $31,000) would be recognized as capital gain, illustrating why the increase-before-decrease ordering rule under Section 705 protects partners from premature gain recognition on smaller distributions", "Distributions reduce basis before income allocations are applied, so a $60,000 distribution would immediately trigger $10,000 of gain regardless of the income allocation"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (C): Under IRC Section 705, outside basis is adjusted by first adding income items (ordinary income of $35,000 and tax-exempt income of $12,000, bringing basis to $97,000), then subtracting distributions and deduction items. With the $15,000 distribution: $97,000 - $15,000 - $8,000 = $74,000. The ordering matters because it maximizes the basis available to absorb distributions without triggering gain. If the distribution were $60,000 instead, basis after income increases would be $97,000, then reduced by $60,000 to $37,000, then by $8,000 deductions to $29,000 — no gain. But if ordering were reversed (distributions first), the $60,000 would exceed the $50,000 starting basis, triggering $10,000 of premature capital gain under Section 731(a). (A) is wrong because ordering is required by Section 705 and affects outcomes. (B) is wrong because with proper ordering, the $60,000 distribution does not exceed basis and no gain is triggered. (D) is wrong because distributions do not precede income adjustments under Section 705.',
  correct_index = 2
WHERE id = 996;

-- ID 997: Advanced Basis Calculations — Recourse vs nonrecourse debt effect on at-risk limitations
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Thornfield Partners has a 50% partner whose share of partnership liabilities includes $100,000 of recourse debt and $80,000 of nonrecourse debt. The partner contributed $40,000 cash at formation and has been allocated $60,000 of cumulative income. In the current year, the partnership allocates a $200,000 loss to this partner. How does the distinction between recourse and nonrecourse debt affect the partner''s ability to deduct this loss under the at-risk rules compared to the basis limitation?',
  choices = '["Both recourse and nonrecourse debt increase the at-risk amount equally, so the partner can deduct the full $200,000 loss under both limitations", "The partner''s outside basis of $280,000 ($40,000 + $60,000 + $100,000 + $80,000) exceeds the $200,000 loss, but the at-risk amount of $200,000 ($40,000 + $60,000 + $100,000 recourse only) exactly equals the loss — the nonrecourse debt increases basis but not the at-risk amount, meaning the loss passes the basis test but is fully at risk only because recourse debt is included", "The partner''s at-risk amount includes both recourse and nonrecourse debt totaling $280,000, so both limitations are satisfied", "The at-risk amount is limited to the $40,000 cash contribution only, so the partner can deduct only $40,000 regardless of debt allocations"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Under IRC Section 752, both recourse and nonrecourse liabilities increase a partner''s outside basis, giving this partner $280,000 ($40,000 cash + $60,000 income + $100,000 recourse + $80,000 nonrecourse). The $200,000 loss passes the Section 704(d) basis limitation. However, the at-risk rules under Section 465 generally exclude nonrecourse debt from the at-risk amount (with an exception for qualified nonrecourse financing on real estate). The at-risk amount is $200,000 ($40,000 + $60,000 + $100,000 recourse). The loss exactly equals the at-risk amount, so the full $200,000 is deductible. This illustrates how the two limitations apply sequentially and how nonrecourse debt creates a gap between basis and at-risk amounts. (A) is wrong because nonrecourse debt generally does not increase the at-risk amount. (C) is wrong for the same reason. (D) is wrong because recourse debt and cumulative income also increase the at-risk amount.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 997;

-- ID 998: Advanced Basis Calculations — Section 754 election effect on buyer vs continuing partners
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Prescott acquires a 25% partnership interest for $300,000 from a retiring partner. The partnership''s total inside basis of assets is $800,000. The partnership has a Section 754 election in effect. How does the Section 743(b) adjustment affect Prescott compared to the continuing partners, and what would happen if no Section 754 election were in place?',
  choices = '["The Section 743(b) adjustment increases the partnership''s total inside basis from $800,000 to $1,000,000, benefiting all partners equally through higher depreciation deductions and lower gains on asset sales", "Without a Section 754 election, Prescott would still receive the basis step-up because the purchase price automatically adjusts the inside basis of partnership assets proportionally", "The Section 743(b) adjustment creates a $100,000 positive basis adjustment ($300,000 purchase price minus $200,000 share of inside basis) that benefits only Prescott — it reduces Prescott''s share of gain on future asset sales without affecting the other partners'' allocations, and without the election, Prescott would face an economic double tax paying $300,000 for the interest but being allocated gain based on the lower $200,000 inside basis", "The adjustment reduces Prescott''s outside basis from $300,000 to $200,000 to match the inside basis, eliminating any discrepancy between the two"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (C): Under IRC Section 743(b), when a Section 754 election is in effect, the purchasing partner receives a special basis adjustment equal to the difference between the purchase price and the partner''s share of the partnership''s inside basis. Prescott''s 25% share of inside basis is $200,000 (25% of $800,000), but the purchase price is $300,000, creating a positive $100,000 adjustment. This adjustment is personal to Prescott — it reduces taxable gain or increases deductions allocated to Prescott on future sales or depreciation of partnership assets without changing anything for other partners. Without the election, Prescott''s outside basis would be $300,000 but the inside basis share would remain $200,000, creating a mismatch where Prescott could be allocated gain on $200,000 of basis despite paying $300,000. (A) is wrong because the adjustment is partner-specific, not partnership-wide. (B) is wrong because without the election, no inside basis adjustment occurs. (D) is wrong because the adjustment increases inside basis, not decreases outside basis.',
  correct_index = 2
WHERE id = 998;

