-- ID 1038: Entity Choice and Planning — Section 199A QBI deduction effect on entity choice
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Ashford Professional Services LLC earns $400,000 of qualified business income (QBI) through a single-member LLC taxed as a sole proprietorship. The owner''s taxable income before the QBI deduction is $290,000 (married filing jointly). A colleague operating an identical practice through a C corporation earns the same $400,000 and pays a 21% corporate rate with no distributions. How does the Section 199A deduction affect the comparative tax burden, and what factor most limits the deduction for the LLC owner?',
  choices = '["The LLC owner receives a 20% QBI deduction of $80,000 with no limitation because taxable income is below the phase-in threshold, reducing effective taxable QBI to $320,000 — the C corporation pays $84,000 in entity-level tax with additional shareholder tax deferred until distribution, making the LLC structure immediately advantageous but the C corporation advantageous if funds are retained long-term", "The LLC owner receives no QBI deduction because professional services are completely excluded from Section 199A regardless of income level", "The LLC owner''s QBI deduction is limited to 50% of W-2 wages paid because the specified service trade or business phase-out has fully eliminated the deduction at this income level", "Both structures produce identical total tax because Section 199A was designed to equalize pass-through and corporate tax rates"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): Under IRC Section 199A, the QBI deduction equals 20% of qualified business income, subject to limitations. For married filing jointly taxpayers with taxable income below the threshold ($364,200 for 2025, adjusted annually), the specified service trade or business (SSTB) limitation and W-2 wage limitation do not apply. At $290,000 taxable income, the LLC owner is below the threshold, so the full 20% deduction of $80,000 applies, reducing taxable income to $210,000 (approximately). The C corporation pays $84,000 in corporate tax (21% of $400,000), with additional tax deferred until distribution. The immediate advantage favors the LLC, but C corporation retention avoids individual-level tax until distribution. (B) is wrong because SSTB limitations only apply above the income threshold. (C) is wrong because the W-2 wage limitation does not apply below the threshold. (D) is wrong because the two structures produce different tax results depending on income level, entity type, and distribution timing.',
  correct_index = 0
WHERE id = 1038;

-- ID 1040: Entity Choice and Planning — S corporation shareholder limit effect on ownership restructuring
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Mercer Holdings is a closely held C corporation with 85 individual shareholders considering S corporation election. The company also has one shareholder that is a single-member LLC owned by a U.S. citizen, one irrevocable trust holding shares for a minor beneficiary, and one venture capital fund (a partnership) holding 5% of shares. Which ownership structure prevents the S election, and how would the company need to restructure to qualify?',
  choices = '["The 88 total shareholders exceed the 75-shareholder limit for S corporations, so the company must reduce its shareholder count before electing", "The single-member LLC is a disregarded entity whose owner counts as the shareholder, and the qualifying trust can be an eligible S corporation shareholder, but the venture capital fund (partnership) is an ineligible shareholder type — the company must redeem the fund''s shares or convert the fund''s interest to an eligible form before the S election can take effect", "The irrevocable trust is an ineligible shareholder because trusts cannot hold S corporation stock under any circumstances", "All current shareholders are eligible because LLCs, trusts, and partnerships can all hold S corporation stock after the 2017 tax reform"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Under IRC Section 1361(b)(1), S corporations may have no more than 100 shareholders, and eligible shareholders are limited to individuals, certain trusts (grantor trusts, qualified subchapter S trusts, electing small business trusts), and estates. Partnerships, corporations, and most entities cannot be S corporation shareholders. The single-member LLC is a disregarded entity under Treas. Reg. 301.7701-3, so its individual owner counts as the shareholder — this is permissible. The irrevocable trust can qualify as an ESBT or QSST if it meets the requirements. However, the venture capital fund structured as a partnership is an ineligible shareholder type. The company must redeem the partnership''s shares or have the partnership distribute the shares to its individual partners before the S election. (A) is wrong because the 100-shareholder limit (not 75) is not exceeded. (C) is wrong because certain trusts are specifically allowed. (D) is wrong because partnerships remain ineligible S corporation shareholders.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 1040;

-- ID 1041: Entity Choice and Planning — Double taxation effect on C corporation distributions vs retention
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Ridgemont Industries, a C corporation, earns $500,000 and is considering distributing $300,000 as dividends versus retaining all earnings. The corporate tax rate is 21% and the shareholder''s qualified dividend rate is 15%. How does the total tax burden compare between distribution and retention, and what additional tax risk does the retention strategy create under the accumulated earnings tax?',
  choices = '["Distribution produces $150,000 total tax ($105,000 corporate + $45,000 shareholder dividend tax), while retention produces only $105,000 corporate tax — retention always saves taxes with no additional risk because the accumulated earnings tax was repealed", "Distribution produces $150,000 total tax, while retention defers the $45,000 shareholder tax but risks a 20% accumulated earnings tax under Section 531 on earnings retained beyond reasonable business needs — if the IRS determines the $300,000 retention lacks a business purpose, the penalty tax of $60,000 would exceed the $45,000 dividend tax that was deferred", "Retention is always preferable because the shareholder tax is permanently eliminated, not just deferred, when earnings are retained at the corporate level", "Distribution and retention produce identical total tax because the accumulated earnings tax automatically offsets any deferral benefit from retention"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): With distribution, total tax is $105,000 corporate tax (21% of $500,000) plus $45,000 dividend tax (15% of $300,000) = $150,000. Retention defers the shareholder-level tax, producing only $105,000 immediately. However, IRC Section 531 imposes a 20% accumulated earnings tax on earnings retained beyond reasonable business needs (approximately $250,000 credit for most corporations). If the IRS challenges the retention as tax-motivated rather than business-driven, the penalty tax on $300,000 would be $60,000 — exceeding the $45,000 deferred dividend tax. This analysis shows that retention''s deferral benefit must be weighed against the Section 531 risk, making the optimal strategy dependent on demonstrable business purpose for retained earnings. (A) is wrong because the accumulated earnings tax under Section 531 remains in effect. (C) is wrong because retained earnings are eventually taxed upon distribution or liquidation. (D) is wrong because the two strategies produce different immediate and long-term tax results.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 1041;

