-- ID 1229: Capital Structure Tax Planning — Section 163(j) ATI calculation effect on interest deductibility
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Cascade Manufacturing has adjusted taxable income (ATI) of $2,000,000 and business interest expense of $900,000 for 2025. The company has no business interest income and no floor plan financing. A competitor, Thornfield Equipment, has identical ATI and interest expense but also has $200,000 of business interest income. How does the Section 163(j) limitation apply differently to each company, and what is the effect on each company''s current-year deductible interest?',
  choices = '["Both companies can deduct the full $900,000 because ATI of $2,000,000 exceeds total interest expense under either scenario", "Cascade can deduct $600,000 (30% of $2,000,000 ATI) with $300,000 carried forward, while Thornfield can deduct $800,000 (30% of $2,000,000 ATI plus $200,000 business interest income) with only $100,000 carried forward — the business interest income directly increases the deduction ceiling, reducing Thornfield''s disallowed amount by $200,000", "Both companies are limited to $600,000 because business interest income does not affect the Section 163(j) calculation", "Cascade must carry forward $900,000 and Thornfield must carry forward $700,000 because the 30% limitation applies to gross revenue, not ATI"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Under IRC Section 163(j), the business interest deduction is limited to the sum of (1) business interest income, (2) 30% of adjusted taxable income, and (3) floor plan financing interest. For Cascade: $0 + (30% x $2,000,000) + $0 = $600,000 deductible, with $300,000 ($900,000 - $600,000) carried forward indefinitely. For Thornfield: $200,000 + (30% x $2,000,000) + $0 = $800,000 deductible, with only $100,000 carried forward. Business interest income is added to the limitation ceiling dollar-for-dollar, directly increasing the amount of business interest expense that can be deducted. This comparison illustrates why companies with interest-bearing assets effectively have a higher interest deduction capacity. (A) is wrong because the 30% ATI limit applies regardless of the relationship between ATI and interest expense. (C) is wrong because business interest income explicitly increases the limitation. (D) is wrong because the 30% applies to ATI, not gross revenue.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 1229;

-- ID 1231: Capital Structure Tax Planning — Section 163(j) limitation with large interest expense
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'GreenCo has 2025 taxable income of $3,000,000, business interest expense of $1,500,000, business interest income of $0, depreciation of $800,000, and amortization of $200,000. Assuming ATI equals taxable income before interest deduction (i.e., $3,000,000), what is the effect of the Section 163(j) limitation on GreenCo''s cash tax position compared to a scenario where GreenCo had structured the same financing as operating leases instead of debt?',
  choices = '["GreenCo can deduct only $900,000 of the $1,500,000 interest (30% of $3,000,000 ATI), carrying forward $600,000 — if the financing had been structured as operating leases, the full lease payments would be deductible as rent expense without a Section 163(j) limitation, resulting in up to $600,000 more in current-year deductions and demonstrating how the interest limitation creates a structural preference for lease financing over debt financing", "GreenCo can deduct the full $1,500,000 because the depreciation and amortization addbacks increase ATI high enough to cover all interest expense", "The Section 163(j) limitation applies equally to interest expense and lease payments, so restructuring as operating leases provides no benefit", "GreenCo deducts $900,000 currently but the $600,000 carryforward is lost if not used within five years, making lease financing preferable only for short-lived assets"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): Under IRC Section 163(j), GreenCo''s deduction limit is 30% of ATI ($3,000,000) = $900,000. The $600,000 excess ($1,500,000 - $900,000) is carried forward indefinitely but provides no current tax benefit. If GreenCo had structured the same financing as operating leases, the lease payments would be characterized as rent expense under Section 162, which is not subject to the Section 163(j) limitation. This means the full payment amount would be currently deductible. The $600,000 difference in current deductions at a 21% rate represents $126,000 in deferred tax savings, illustrating how Section 163(j) creates an economic incentive to use lease structures over debt. (B) is wrong because for tax years beginning after 2021, depreciation and amortization are no longer added back to ATI. (C) is wrong because Section 163(j) applies only to interest expense, not lease or rent payments. (D) is wrong because the carryforward period is indefinite under current law.',
  correct_index = 0
WHERE id = 1231;

-- ID 1232: Capital Structure Tax Planning — Debt vs equity characterization effect on below-market interest
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Hawkins holds both 60% of the stock and 60% of the corporate notes of Ridgemont Corp. The notes bear a below-market interest rate of 1% when the applicable federal rate is 5%. The IRS is examining whether the notes should be reclassified as equity. How would reclassification from debt to equity affect Ridgemont Corp''s tax position, and what factor most strongly supports the IRS''s reclassification argument?',
  choices = '["Reclassification would have no tax effect because interest payments and dividend payments receive the same tax treatment at both the corporate and shareholder levels", "Reclassification would convert deductible interest payments into nondeductible dividend distributions, increasing Ridgemont''s taxable income — the below-market interest rate most strongly supports reclassification because it indicates the ''lender'' is not seeking a market return on debt but rather disguising an equity investment to obtain interest deductions at the corporate level", "Reclassification would benefit Ridgemont because dividend payments to a 60% corporate shareholder qualify for the dividends-received deduction while interest income does not", "The 60% ownership alone automatically triggers reclassification under Section 385 regardless of the interest rate or other terms"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Under IRC Section 385 and related case law, the IRS examines multiple factors to determine whether an instrument labeled as debt is actually equity. These include the debt-to-equity ratio, whether payments are contingent on earnings, the presence or absence of a fixed maturity date, and whether the interest rate is at arm''s length. A below-market interest rate of 1% versus a 5% AFR is strong evidence that the instrument does not represent a genuine creditor-debtor relationship — a true lender would demand a market return. Reclassification converts interest deductions into nondeductible dividends, directly increasing Ridgemont''s taxable income by the amount previously deducted as interest. (A) is wrong because interest is deductible to the corporation while dividends are not, creating a significant tax difference. (C) is wrong because the dividends-received deduction applies to corporate shareholders, not to the issuing corporation, and does not offset the lost deduction. (D) is wrong because ownership percentage alone does not trigger automatic reclassification; the IRS must analyze the totality of factors.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 1232;

