-- ID 1248: Related Party Transactions — Scope of Section 267 related party definitions
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Dawson owns 40% of Ashford Corp and 70% of Meridian Corp. He sells equipment with an adjusted basis of $120,000 to Ashford Corp for $85,000, realizing a $35,000 loss. In a separate transaction, his wife sells land with a basis of $60,000 to Meridian Corp for $45,000, realizing a $15,000 loss. How does Section 267 apply differently to these two transactions, and what is the combined effect on the family''s recognized losses?',
  choices = '["Both losses are disallowed because Dawson''s direct and constructive ownership exceeds 50% in both corporations under the Section 267 attribution rules — Dawson is attributed his wife''s 0% direct ownership in Meridian, but his own 70% direct ownership already triggers the related party rule, and his wife is attributed his 40% in Ashford plus her own 0%, which does not exceed 50%, making the Ashford loss actually allowable", "Dawson''s $35,000 loss on the sale to Ashford Corp is allowed because his 40% ownership does not exceed the 50% threshold, but his wife''s $15,000 loss on the sale to Meridian Corp is disallowed because she is attributed Dawson''s 70% ownership under Section 267(c), giving her constructive ownership exceeding 50% — the combined effect is that only $35,000 of the $50,000 total losses is deductible", "Both losses are fully deductible because Section 267 only applies to sales between an individual and a corporation where the individual directly owns more than 50%", "Both losses are disallowed because spouses are always treated as a single taxpayer for all loss limitation purposes regardless of ownership percentages"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Under IRC Section 267(b), losses are disallowed on sales between an individual and a corporation in which the individual owns (directly or constructively) more than 50% of the stock. Section 267(c) attributes stock owned by a spouse, creating constructive ownership. For Dawson''s sale to Ashford Corp: Dawson directly owns 40%. His wife owns 0% of Ashford, so his constructive ownership remains 40% — below the 50% threshold. The $35,000 loss is allowed. For his wife''s sale to Meridian Corp: she directly owns 0%, but is attributed Dawson''s 70% under Section 267(c)(2), giving her constructive ownership of 70% — exceeding 50%. The $15,000 loss is disallowed. The combined result is $35,000 deductible out of $50,000 total. (A) incorrectly concludes both are disallowed. (C) is wrong because constructive ownership rules apply, not just direct ownership. (D) is wrong because Section 267 applies the more-than-50% threshold with attribution, not a blanket spousal rule.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 1248;

-- ID 1249: Related Party Transactions — Section 267(a)(2) matching rule effect on timing of deductions
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Ridgemont Corp (accrual basis) accrues a $75,000 year-end bonus to Garcia, its 80% shareholder who reports on the cash basis. The bonus is accrued on December 31, 2025, but not paid until March 15, 2026 — within the 2.5-month window. In a separate transaction, Ridgemont accrues a $50,000 consulting fee to an unrelated cash-basis contractor on December 31, 2025, also paid on March 15, 2026. How does Section 267(a)(2) affect the timing of these two deductions differently?',
  choices = '["Both deductions are disallowed entirely because accrual-basis corporations cannot deduct payments to cash-basis payees", "Both deductions are deferred until 2026 because the cash-basis payees do not include the income until payment is received, and Section 267(a)(2) applies to all accrued expenses", "The $75,000 bonus deduction is allowed in 2025 because payment within 2.5 months satisfies the Section 267(a)(2) matching requirement for related parties, while the $50,000 consulting fee is also deductible in 2025 because Section 267(a)(2) does not apply to unrelated parties — the matching rule only restricts timing when the payee is a related party and payment extends beyond the filing deadline", "The $75,000 bonus is deferred to 2026 regardless of when paid because all related-party transactions are deferred, while the $50,000 fee is deductible in 2025 under normal accrual rules"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (C): Under IRC Section 267(a)(2), an accrual-basis taxpayer cannot deduct an expense owed to a related cash-basis payee until the payee includes the amount in income. An 80% shareholder is a related party under Section 267(b). However, Treas. Reg. 1.267(a)-3 provides that if the accrual-basis payor pays the related party within 2.5 months after year-end (by March 15 for a calendar-year taxpayer), the deduction is allowed in the accrual year because the payee will include the income in the following year when received. Since Garcia is paid by March 15, 2026, the $75,000 is deductible in 2025. The $50,000 fee to the unrelated contractor is governed by normal accrual rules — Section 267(a)(2) does not apply because there is no related-party relationship. Both are deductible in 2025 but for different reasons. (A) is wrong because the deductions are not permanently disallowed. (B) is wrong because Section 267(a)(2) only applies to related parties. (D) is wrong because payment within 2.5 months satisfies the matching requirement.',
  correct_index = 2
WHERE id = 1249;

-- ID 1250: Related Party Transactions — Section 1239 ordinary income recharacterization analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Torres sells a warehouse (adjusted basis $200,000, FMV $350,000) to her 100%-owned S corporation for $350,000. The warehouse is depreciable real property in the hands of the corporation. Torres''s friend, Nakamura, sells an identical warehouse (same basis and FMV) to an unrelated buyer for $350,000. Both warehouses were held as capital assets by the sellers. How does Section 1239 change the character of Torres''s gain compared to Nakamura''s, and what is the policy rationale?',
  choices = '["Both sellers recognize $150,000 of Section 1231 gain, potentially taxed at long-term capital gains rates, because the character of gain depends on the seller''s holding period and use, not the buyer''s identity", "Torres recognizes $150,000 of ordinary income under Section 1239 while Nakamura recognizes $150,000 of Section 1231 gain eligible for capital gains rates — Section 1239 prevents Torres from converting ordinary depreciation deductions at the corporate level into preferential capital gain at the shareholder level, closing a loophole where related parties could shift income character across entities", "Torres recognizes $150,000 of ordinary income and Nakamura also recognizes ordinary income because all sales of depreciable real property produce ordinary income regardless of the buyer", "Section 1239 reclassifies Torres''s gain as a dividend rather than ordinary income because the buyer is her controlled corporation"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Under IRC Section 1239, gain on the sale of depreciable property between related parties (including a taxpayer and a more-than-50%-owned entity) is recharacterized as ordinary income rather than Section 1231 gain. Torres''s $150,000 gain is ordinary income, taxed at her marginal rate (up to 37%). Nakamura''s identical gain qualifies as Section 1231 gain, potentially taxed at the 15% or 20% capital gains rate. The policy rationale is that the S corporation will depreciate the $350,000 purchase price, generating ordinary deductions. Without Section 1239, Torres would receive capital gain treatment on the sale while her corporation claims ordinary depreciation deductions — a mismatch that effectively converts ordinary income into capital gain within the same economic unit. (A) is wrong because Section 1239 specifically overrides Section 1231 for related-party sales. (C) is wrong because depreciable real property sales to unrelated parties qualify for Section 1231 treatment. (D) is wrong because Section 1239 produces ordinary income, not dividend treatment.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 1250;

