-- ID 5492: Entity Formation and Restructuring — Type C reorganization continuity of interest analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Prescott Industries acquires substantially all of the assets of Waverly Corp solely in exchange for Prescott''s voting stock in a Type C reorganization. Waverly then liquidates and distributes the Prescott stock to its shareholders. In a separate transaction, Kendrick Corp acquires the assets of Summit Corp for 70% Prescott voting stock and 30% cash. Both transactions are intended to qualify as Type C reorganizations. How does the ''substantially all'' and solely-for-voting-stock requirement affect the qualification of each transaction?',
  choices = '["Both transactions qualify because any combination of stock and cash is permitted in a Type C reorganization as long as the acquiring corporation obtains at least 50% of the target''s assets", "The Prescott-Waverly transaction qualifies because it uses solely voting stock, but the Kendrick-Summit transaction fails the Type C ''solely for voting stock'' requirement — although a limited boot relaxation allows up to 20% non-stock consideration, the 30% cash exceeds this threshold, potentially disqualifying the reorganization and causing Summit''s shareholders to recognize gain on the entire transaction", "Both transactions fail because Type C reorganizations require the target corporation to remain in existence after the asset transfer, and both targets liquidate", "The Prescott-Waverly transaction fails because ''substantially all'' requires 100% of assets, and no target corporation can transfer exactly 100% while retaining enough assets to complete the liquidation"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Under IRC Section 368(a)(1)(C), a Type C reorganization requires the acquiring corporation to obtain substantially all of the target''s assets solely in exchange for voting stock. The ''solely for voting stock'' requirement has a limited boot relaxation under Section 368(a)(2)(B) that allows up to 20% of the total consideration to be non-stock (cash or other property), but only if the acquiring corporation also obtains at least 80% of the target''s assets for voting stock. The Prescott-Waverly transaction satisfies both requirements: 100% voting stock and substantially all assets. The Kendrick-Summit transaction uses 30% cash, exceeding the 20% boot relaxation, and likely fails to qualify. If disqualified, the transaction would be treated as a taxable asset purchase, and Summit''s shareholders would recognize gain. (A) is wrong because Type C has strict solely-for-voting-stock requirements. (C) is wrong because target liquidation is generally required, not prohibited, in Type C reorganizations. (D) is wrong because ''substantially all'' does not mean exactly 100% — IRS rulings generally accept 70-90% of assets.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 5492;

-- ID 5494: Entity Formation and Restructuring — Boot in reorganization: gain recognition analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'In a qualifying Type A reorganization, shareholder Vasquez exchanges Thornfield Corp stock with a basis of $60,000 for acquiring corporation stock worth $80,000 and $20,000 cash. Shareholder Park exchanges Thornfield stock with a basis of $115,000 for the same package of $80,000 stock and $20,000 cash. How does the boot recognition rule under Section 356 apply differently to these two shareholders, and why?',
  choices = '["Both shareholders recognize $20,000 of gain because boot received always equals gain recognized in a reorganization, regardless of the shareholder''s built-in gain or loss", "Vasquez recognizes $20,000 gain (the lesser of $40,000 realized gain and $20,000 boot received), while Park recognizes neither gain nor loss despite receiving $20,000 boot — Section 356(c) provides that boot does not trigger gain when the shareholder has a realized loss ($15,000 loss here), and the loss itself is not recognized, demonstrating that the boot rule is asymmetric between gain and loss positions", "Vasquez recognizes $40,000 gain and Park recognizes the full $15,000 loss because all realized gains and losses must be recognized when boot is present", "Both shareholders defer all gain and loss because the boot amount is less than 50% of total consideration, triggering automatic deferral under the de minimis boot rule"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Under IRC Section 356(a), when a shareholder receives boot in a reorganization, gain is recognized to the extent of the lesser of the realized gain or the boot received. Vasquez has a realized gain of $40,000 ($100,000 total value minus $60,000 basis), so gain recognized is the lesser of $40,000 or $20,000 boot = $20,000. Park has a realized loss of $15,000 ($100,000 total value minus $115,000 basis). Under Section 356(c), no loss is recognized in a reorganization regardless of boot received. The $20,000 boot does not convert Park''s loss into a recognized amount — it simply cannot trigger recognition when the overall position is a loss. This asymmetry prevents taxpayers from engineering recognized losses through reorganization structures while ensuring that gain shareholders cannot use the tax-free reorganization to shelter boot income. (A) is wrong because gain recognized is capped at the lesser of realized gain or boot. (C) is wrong because losses are never recognized in reorganizations under Section 356(c). (D) is wrong because there is no de minimis boot exception in the Code.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 5494;

-- ID 5495: Entity Formation and Restructuring — Type E recapitalization: stock-for-debt exchange analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Thornfield Corp''s board authorizes a recapitalization under Section 368(a)(1)(E), exchanging all outstanding $100 par value preferred stock for new common stock. Shareholder Henderson, who holds preferred stock with a basis of $80,000 and a par value of $100,000, receives common stock worth $120,000. In a separate recapitalization, shareholder Webb exchanges bonds with a face value of $100,000 and a basis of $95,000 for common stock worth $110,000. How does the tax treatment differ between the stock-for-stock and bond-for-stock exchanges in these recapitalizations?',
  choices = '["Both exchanges are fully tax-free because all recapitalizations under Section 368(a)(1)(E) provide complete nonrecognition regardless of the types of securities exchanged", "Henderson''s stock-for-stock exchange qualifies for tax-free treatment under the recapitalization rules with no gain recognized and a substituted basis of $80,000 in the new common stock, while Webb''s bond-for-stock exchange triggers recognition of $15,000 gain because bonds are ''securities'' and the exchange of debt for equity creates a deemed satisfaction of the debt obligation at fair market value", "Both exchanges produce taxable gain because any change in the character of securities (preferred to common, or debt to equity) is treated as a taxable disposition", "Henderson recognizes $40,000 gain and Webb recognizes $15,000 gain because recapitalizations are only tax-free when the securities exchanged are of identical type and value"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Under IRC Section 368(a)(1)(E), a recapitalization (Type E reorganization) allows tax-free exchanges of stock for stock within the same corporation. Henderson''s exchange of preferred stock for common stock is a qualifying stock-for-stock recapitalization — no gain is recognized and the basis carries over at $80,000 under Section 358. Webb''s exchange of bonds for stock is more complex. While bonds can be ''securities'' in a reorganization, a corporation''s exchange of its own debt for its own equity is generally treated as a satisfaction of the debt obligation. Under Section 354, the bondholder may recognize gain to the extent the stock FMV exceeds the bond''s adjusted basis ($110,000 - $95,000 = $15,000). The distinction illustrates that recapitalizations involving debt instruments carry different tax consequences than pure equity exchanges. (A) is wrong because debt-for-equity exchanges in recapitalizations can trigger gain recognition. (C) is wrong because stock-for-stock exchanges within the same corporation are specifically permitted. (D) is wrong because stock-for-stock recapitalizations are tax-free even when the stock type changes.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 5495;

