-- ID 5474: Multi-Jurisdictional Tax Planning — Market-based vs cost-of-performance sourcing comparison
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Ashford Consulting is based in State Y and provides strategy consulting to a client headquartered in State X, with project work performed 80% in State Y and 20% in State X. State X uses market-based sourcing, while State Y uses cost-of-performance sourcing. Both states have a 7% income tax rate. The consulting contract generates $500,000 in revenue. How does the sourcing method difference affect which state taxes this income, and what is the risk of double taxation?',
  choices = '["State X sources the full $500,000 to State X under market-based rules (customer location), while State Y sources the full $500,000 to State Y under cost-of-performance rules (80% performance exceeds the majority threshold) — both states claim 100% of the income, creating $70,000 of potential double taxation that can only be mitigated by credits or apportionment adjustments", "Only State X taxes the income because market-based sourcing takes precedence over cost-of-performance sourcing under federal preemption rules", "Only State Y taxes the income because the work is predominantly performed there and the cost-of-performance method always overrides market-based sourcing", "Both states source 50% of the income, splitting the tax burden equally under the Uniform Division of Income for Tax Purposes Act"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): Under market-based sourcing, service revenue is sourced to the state where the customer receives the benefit — State X claims 100% of the $500,000. Under cost-of-performance sourcing, revenue is sourced to the state where the greater proportion of income-producing activity occurs. Since 80% of work is in State Y, the ''all-or-nothing'' cost-of-performance rule sources 100% to State Y. Both states independently claim jurisdiction over the full $500,000, creating potential double taxation of $70,000 (2 x 7% x $500,000). There is no federal mechanism that forces states to adopt the same sourcing method, so taxpayers must rely on state-level credits for taxes paid to other states, which are not always available or complete. This sourcing mismatch is a key risk in multistate tax planning. (B) is wrong because there is no federal preemption of state sourcing rules. (C) is wrong because each state applies its own method independently. (D) is wrong because UDITPA provides model rules but states adopt different versions, and there is no mandatory 50/50 split.',
  correct_index = 0
WHERE id = 5474;

-- ID 5478: Multi-Jurisdictional Tax Planning — UDITPA business vs nonbusiness income classification effect
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Ridgemont Manufacturing is domiciled in State A and has operations in States B and C. The company earns $2,000,000 in operating income from manufacturing (apportioned across all three states) and $300,000 in rental income from a building in State B that is unrelated to manufacturing operations. Under UDITPA, how does the classification of the rental income as business versus nonbusiness income affect which states can tax it and the total state tax burden?',
  choices = '["If classified as business income, the $300,000 is included in the apportionable tax base and divided among States A, B, and C based on the apportionment formula — if classified as nonbusiness income, the $300,000 is allocated entirely to State B (the situs state) and excluded from apportionment, potentially increasing or decreasing total tax depending on each state''s rate and Ridgemont''s apportionment factors", "Business and nonbusiness income are always taxed identically because UDITPA requires all income to be apportioned regardless of character", "Nonbusiness income is exempt from state taxation entirely, so the classification determines whether the $300,000 is taxed or untaxed", "The rental income is automatically classified as nonbusiness income because real property income is always allocated to the situs state under UDITPA without exception"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): Under UDITPA (Uniform Division of Income for Tax Purposes Act), business income is apportioned among all states where the taxpayer has nexus using the apportionment formula (typically based on sales, payroll, and property factors). Nonbusiness income is allocated to a specific state — rental income from real property is allocated to the state where the property is located (State B). The classification matters because if the $300,000 is business income, each state taxes its apportioned share. If nonbusiness income, only State B taxes it. The total tax impact depends on the relative rates: if State B has a higher rate than the blended apportioned rate, nonbusiness classification increases total tax. If State B''s rate is lower, it decreases total tax. (B) is wrong because UDITPA explicitly distinguishes between apportionable business income and allocable nonbusiness income. (C) is wrong because nonbusiness income is taxed by the allocation state. (D) is wrong because rental income can be business income if the property is functionally integrated with the taxpayer''s trade or business.',
  correct_index = 0
WHERE id = 5478;

-- ID 5479: Multi-Jurisdictional Tax Planning — Pass-through entity tax election SALT cap workaround analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Waverly Consulting LLC has three members and earns $900,000 of net income in 2025. State Z offers a pass-through entity (PTE) tax election at a 5% rate as a workaround for the federal $10,000 SALT deduction cap. If the members elect PTE treatment, the entity pays $45,000 in state tax and each member receives a corresponding federal tax credit or deduction. Compare the tax effect of the PTE election versus having each member pay state tax individually, assuming each member''s share is $300,000 and each has already exceeded the $10,000 SALT cap from other sources.',
  choices = '["The PTE election has no tax effect because the $10,000 SALT cap applies equally to entity-level and individual-level state tax payments", "Without the PTE election, each member pays $15,000 in state tax individually ($300,000 x 5%) but cannot deduct it federally because they have already exceeded the $10,000 SALT cap — with the PTE election, the $45,000 entity-level tax is deductible against the entity''s income before it flows through to the members, effectively converting a nondeductible individual SALT payment into a deductible business expense that reduces each member''s K-1 income by $15,000, saving approximately $5,550 per member at the 37% federal rate", "The PTE election doubles the state tax burden because both the entity and the members must pay state tax on the same income", "The PTE election only benefits members in the 24% bracket or lower because the SALT cap does not apply to higher-income taxpayers"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): The PTE tax election is designed to circumvent the $10,000 SALT deduction cap imposed by IRC Section 164(b)(6). Without the election, each member pays $15,000 in state tax and receives no federal deduction because they have already exceeded the $10,000 cap. With the election, the entity pays $45,000 in state tax, which reduces the entity''s taxable income before it flows through on K-1s. Each member''s allocable share of income drops from $300,000 to $285,000, and the member receives a state credit or deduction offsetting the entity-level payment. The net federal benefit per member is $15,000 x 37% = $5,550, representing the value of the previously nondeductible state tax. The IRS has acknowledged the validity of PTE elections in Notice 2020-75. (A) is wrong because the SALT cap applies to individual itemized deductions, not entity-level business deductions. (C) is wrong because the PTE tax replaces, rather than supplements, the individual state tax obligation. (D) is wrong because the benefit increases with the marginal rate — higher-bracket taxpayers benefit more.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 5479;

