-- ID 1024: Entity Formation and Liquidation — Section 332 subsidiary liquidation: parent vs minority shareholder treatment
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Ashford Corp owns 85% of Waverly Inc., and an unrelated investor, Chen, owns the remaining 15%. Waverly completely liquidates, distributing assets with a total FMV of $2,000,000 and an adjusted basis of $1,200,000. Ashford''s stock basis in Waverly is $800,000 and Chen''s stock basis is $180,000. How does the tax treatment of the liquidating distribution differ between Ashford (the parent) and Chen (the minority shareholder), and why does the Code treat them differently?',
  choices = '["Both Ashford and Chen recognize no gain or loss because all complete liquidations are tax-free events under Section 331", "Ashford recognizes gain on the difference between FMV received and stock basis, while Chen receives a carryover basis in the assets — the minority shareholder receives preferential treatment because of the smaller ownership stake", "Ashford recognizes no gain or loss under Section 332 and takes a carryover basis in Waverly''s assets under Section 334(b)(1), while Chen recognizes a $120,000 capital gain ($300,000 FMV of 15% share minus $180,000 stock basis) under Section 331 and takes a FMV basis in assets received — Section 332 grants nonrecognition only to the controlling parent to preserve the corporate group''s tax attributes, while minority shareholders are treated as disposing of their investment", "Both shareholders are taxed identically because Section 332 applies to all shareholders of a liquidating subsidiary regardless of ownership percentage"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (C): Under IRC Section 332, a parent corporation that owns at least 80% of a subsidiary''s stock recognizes no gain or loss on a complete liquidation. The parent takes a carryover basis in the received assets under Section 334(b)(1), preserving the subsidiary''s built-in gains for future recognition. Chen, as a minority shareholder (below 80%), is treated under the general liquidation rules of Section 331 — the distribution is treated as a sale of stock, producing a capital gain of $120,000 (15% x $2,000,000 = $300,000 FMV minus $180,000 basis). Chen takes a FMV basis in assets received. The policy rationale is that Section 332 treats the parent-subsidiary liquidation as a mere reorganization of the corporate structure, while minority shareholders are cashing out their investment. (A) is wrong because Section 331 taxes minority shareholders. (B) reverses the treatment. (D) is wrong because Section 332 nonrecognition requires at least 80% ownership.',
  correct_index = 2
WHERE id = 1024;

