-- ID 912: Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules — Rental loss allowance phase-out analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Thornfield, a married taxpayer filing jointly, actively participates in two rental properties. Property A generates a $15,000 loss and Property B generates a $10,000 loss, for a total of $25,000 in rental losses. Thornfield''s AGI is $125,000 before considering rental losses. A colleague, Vasquez, has identical rental losses but an AGI of $90,000. How does the Section 469(i) phase-out affect the deductible rental loss for each taxpayer, and what planning implication does this create?',
  choices = '["Both taxpayers can deduct the full $25,000 because the rental loss allowance permits up to $25,000 of losses regardless of AGI for active participants", "Thornfield can deduct $12,500 because the $25,000 allowance is reduced by 50% of AGI exceeding $100,000 ($125,000 - $100,000 = $25,000 excess x 50% = $12,500 phase-out), leaving $12,500 deductible, while Vasquez can deduct the full $25,000 because AGI of $90,000 is below the $100,000 phase-out threshold — this creates a planning incentive for Thornfield to reduce AGI below $100,000 through retirement contributions or other above-the-line deductions to recover the full $25,000 allowance", "Both taxpayers must suspend all rental losses because the passive activity rules prohibit deducting rental losses against nonpassive income under all circumstances", "Thornfield can deduct $0 because any AGI above $100,000 completely eliminates the rental loss allowance, while Vasquez deducts the full $25,000"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Under IRC Section 469(i), taxpayers who actively participate in rental real estate may deduct up to $25,000 of rental losses against nonpassive income. This allowance phases out by 50 cents for every dollar of AGI exceeding $100,000, and is fully eliminated at $150,000 AGI. For Thornfield (AGI $125,000): excess AGI = $25,000, phase-out = $25,000 x 50% = $12,500, remaining allowance = $25,000 - $12,500 = $12,500 deductible, with $12,500 suspended. For Vasquez (AGI $90,000): no phase-out applies, full $25,000 is deductible. The planning implication is significant: if Thornfield can reduce AGI by $25,000 through 401(k) contributions, HSA contributions, or other above-the-line deductions, the full $25,000 rental allowance is restored, creating a double tax benefit. (A) is wrong because the phase-out begins at $100,000 AGI. (C) is wrong because Section 469(i) is a specific exception for active rental participants. (D) is wrong because the phase-out is gradual, not a cliff at $100,000.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 912;

COMMIT;
